From: Pavel Snajdr <snajpa@snajpa.net>
Subject: [PATCH] NFS: tolerate a cold NLM client during sysfs shutdown

An NFSv3 mount allocates an nlm_host before its first remote lock, but
nlm_host.h_rpcclnt is still NULL until then. The legacy server shutdown
attribute passes that pointer to shutdown_client(), which unconditionally
dereferences it. A privileged cancellation write can therefore crash the
host even when the mount has never used NLM.

There are no RPC tasks to cancel when the client has not been allocated.
Leave the server's terminal state handling unchanged and skip only the
absent RPC client. The native .110 and cumulative .95 candidates already
handle this through their serialized NLM shutdown helper; this compatibility
fix is for the selected .109 kernel only.

--- a/fs/nfs/sysfs.c
+++ b/fs/nfs/sysfs.c
@@ -228,4 +228,7 @@ static void shutdown_client(struct rpc_clnt *clnt)
 {
-	clnt->cl_shutdown = 1;
-	rpc_cancel_tasks(clnt, -EIO, shutdown_match_client, NULL);
+	/* There is nothing to cancel if the client is absent. */
+	if (!clnt)
+		return;
+	clnt->cl_shutdown = 1;
+	rpc_cancel_tasks(clnt, -EIO, shutdown_match_client, NULL);
 }
